\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Illustrative completion-certified rollout ADP, with initial state $s=(0,\mathbf  0,\mathbf  0,3)$. I. Solid edges are admissible, edge labels are stage costs, and $G_j$ lists visible targets. The dashed transfer is excluded. II. At $d=2$, rollout selects zero-gain connector $c_2$ for total cost 4, versus base-policy cost 8; action 3 has no certified completion. III. The successor $s'=(2,\mathbf  0,\mathbf  e_2,2)$ retains a cost-3 completion. Counts indicate covered required targets; $J_{\mathrm  {rollout}}$ is realized graph cost. The example illustrates Propositions~\ref {prop:rollout-viability} and~\ref {prop:base-bound}. Assigned costs and audit flags are illustrative, not experimental HCW data; the primary study uses $d=3$.}}{6}{figure.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:adp-architecture}{{2}{6}{Illustrative completion-certified rollout ADP, with initial state $s=(0,\mathbf 0,\mathbf 0,3)$. I. Solid edges are admissible, edge labels are stage costs, and $G_j$ lists visible targets. The dashed transfer is excluded. II. At $d=2$, rollout selects zero-gain connector $c_2$ for total cost 4, versus base-policy cost 8; action 3 has no certified completion. III. The successor $s'=(2,\mathbf 0,\mathbf e_2,2)$ retains a cost-3 completion. Counts indicate covered required targets; $J_{\mathrm {rollout}}$ is realized graph cost. The example illustrates Propositions~\ref {prop:rollout-viability} and~\ref {prop:base-bound}. Assigned costs and audit flags are illustrative, not experimental HCW data; the primary study uses $d=3$}{figure.38}{}}
